WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.790
As the novel coronavirus

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:04.120
continues to spread worldwide,

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.260
one common theory is that the pandemic

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.800
will die down when warmer weather arrives.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.212
Donald Trump: Looks
like by April, you know,

00:00:10.212 --> 00:00:11.840
in theory, when it gets a little warmer,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.710
it miraculously goes away.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.130
I hope that's true.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:16.220
Narrator: But the answer

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.540
is a little more complicated than that.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.890
So, why do so many
people think that summer

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.720
will stop the COVID-19 spread?

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.810
Seasonal patterns are commonplace

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.140
for many infectious diseases.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.320
Take the flu, for example.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.380
Jeffrey Shaman: We get these
big surges of seasonal flu

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.260
that peak in January and February

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.770
in the Northern Hemisphere

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.603
and then the opposite side of the year,

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:40.330
in July and August, in
the Southern Hemisphere,

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:43.050
places like Australia,
New Zealand, South Africa.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.870
So the question has been why,

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.160
and there are three groups of hypotheses

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.940
that have tried to explain this.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.550
Narrator: The first of these hypotheses

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.780
is human behavior.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.350
The flu spreads more in colder seasons,

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.760
when people are more likely
to gather in enclosed spaces,

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:02.270
and lowers once people venture outside.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:05.173
However, COVID-19 appears to spread

00:01:05.173 --> 00:01:07.430
much more efficiently than the flu,

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.840
which is why strict
physical-distancing laws

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.580
have been enforced,

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.795
but also why you may have heard experts

00:01:13.795 --> 00:01:16.030
warn of a rise in cases

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.550
once people go back to
their offices, schools

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.330
and normal life.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:24.030
The second hypothesis has to
do with your immune system.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.120
Immune systems have
been found to be better

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.930
at fighting respiratory viruses

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.730
during longer summer days,

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.550
which also increase the production

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.250
of the immunity booster vitamin D.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.820
So when summer rolls around,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.160
people might have a better
chance of fighting off

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.890
the coronavirus as well.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.000
And also why you may have seen suggestions

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.190
during this pandemic to
spend time safely outdoors,

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.060
for example, on a balcony
or in your garden,

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.000
or even to take vitamin D supplements.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.945
But people have focused on the flu's

00:01:53.945 --> 00:01:57.223
third seasonality
hypothesis, the environment.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.360
That's because both COVID-19 and the flu

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:02.880
are respiratory diseases

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.510
that can be spread through droplets.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:06.926
Once these droplets have been

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:08.760
coughed, sneezed, or spat out,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.460
they're in the hands of the environment

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:12.710
until they reach a new victim.

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:15.090
Studies have found that
the transmission levels

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.635
decrease when the
influenza virus encounters

00:02:17.635 --> 00:02:20.020
a humid and hot environment.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.490
That's the opposite of
what we see during winter,

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:26.300
where cold temperatures go
hand in hand with dry air.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.620
One study found that when humidity levels

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.480
were at 23% after 60 minutes,

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.290
70% to 77% of the flu virus survived.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.680
But when humidity increased to 43%,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:42.180
only 14% to 22% of the flu virus particles

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.230
survived in the same amount of time.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.765
Shaman: This humidity change seems to be

00:02:46.765 --> 00:02:48.780
modulating the survival of the virus,

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.820
such that it doesn't last
as long in the summertime

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.433
and it lasts a much longer time

00:02:53.433 --> 00:02:55.510
when it's drier in the wintertime.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:59.430
And that may encourage and
allow the transmission,

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.240
and hence we see these
surges in the wintertime.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.010
Narrator: And scientists
have a few answers

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.510
as to why this is the case.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.190
The influenza virus is
a simple genetic code

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.290
surrounded by a protein
layer and a fatty envelope,

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.090
similar to SARS-CoV-2,

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:16.470
the virus that caused COVID-19.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.750
But once it leaves the body,

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.520
a layer of water encapsulates it as well.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.520
Some scientists believe that
in high-humidity environments,

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.670
the virus picks up more water particles

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.420
and becomes heavier,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.840
which forces it to drop out of the air,

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.480
lowering the chance of transmission.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.900
In dry conditions, like in winter,

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.850
those same droplets evaporate,

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:40.202
making the virus much lighter
and airborne for longer.

00:03:40.202 --> 00:03:41.755
But that evaporation has another

00:03:41.755 --> 00:03:43.600
positive transmission effect.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.200
The water droplet and virus are not alone.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.080
They are also joined by salts,

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.830
and one study found that
as the water droplets

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.740
evaporate in the dry air,

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.170
those salts are removed,

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.970
increasing the virus' stability

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.270
and probability of transmission.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.750
But it's not just humidity
that has an impact

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.410
on the influenza virus.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:05.220
Researchers have found the virus to be

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.990
much more robust in
colder temperatures too,

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:12.410
lasting far longer than when
exposed to higher temperatures.

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:15.820
So, by now, you may be
thinking the flu and COVID-19

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.500
are both respiratory infections

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.520
with somewhat similar structures,

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.840
so surely they will follow

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.160
similar seasonal patterns as well.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.503
But let's be clear:

00:04:24.503 --> 00:04:26.160
They're not the same.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.730
Not only in transmission rates,
symptoms, and death rates,

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.450
but also in the length of
time that they have existed

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.730
in human populations.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.550
This is pretty important to consider,

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:37.650
especially when you look at

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.390
how virus transmissions slow down.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:41.780
Micaela Martinez: At the
beginning of the high season,

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.660
you have some big susceptible
pool of individuals,

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:48.670
and then as that epidemic
rose and cases climbed,

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.740
that susceptible pool's
getting smaller and smaller

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.090
and smaller and smaller,

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:53.730
so when you get up here at the top,

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:56.830
you have fewer people
that can get infected.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.800
Narrator: You can think of
this like kindling for a fire.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.300
The longer the fire burns,

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.030
the more fuel is used,

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.020
so the less kindling you have,

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.920
and the fire will eventually
begin to burn out.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.050
Both the smaller susceptible pool

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.099
and the onset of the low season,

00:05:12.099 --> 00:05:13.782
such as a rise in humidity,

00:05:13.782 --> 00:05:15.610
dictate when a disease's high season

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:17.210
begins to turn around.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:19.335
Martinez: That's very different from

00:05:19.335 --> 00:05:21.101
a pandemic situation,

00:05:21.101 --> 00:05:23.830
where you very well may have

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:26.130
seasonal changes in transmission,

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:30.300
but then that's having to
work essentially all by itself

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.770
if there's not a shrinking
of the susceptible pool

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.690
that comes along with it.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:37.800
Narrator: For a new viral
pandemic like the current one,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.250
the susceptible pool is huge.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:43.020
Populations haven't
developed immunity to it

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.020
like they have against, say, the flu.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.360
And when you throw in a lack
of vaccines and treatments,

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.330
the fuel for the fire is almost endless.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:54.250
This isn't to say that
COVID-19 won't be hindered

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:55.820
at all by seasonality.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:58.460
One initial study from
China showed decreases

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.160
in SARS-CoV-2 transmission

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.470
along with increases in humidity.

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:03.910
However, another study

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:06.433
wasn't able to establish that connection.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:09.370
And both should be taken
with a grain of salt

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:10.550
in the first place,

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:13.130
as neither has been produced
in a lab setting yet.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:14.580
What could give a better insight

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:15.650
are the ways in which

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.470
the four known common human coronaviruses,

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.960
which are partly responsible
for the common cold,

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.850
react to seasons.

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:25.830
Shaman: So, there's evidence
from other coronaviruses,

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:27.978
and all four of them do this,

00:06:27.978 --> 00:06:31.110
that they have this
pronounced seasonality.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.360
So there is this enticing possibility

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.743
that has to be considered seriously

00:06:35.743 --> 00:06:37.018
and not dismissed

00:06:37.018 --> 00:06:41.130
that this novel coronavirus
may also have this property

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.910
because it's related to them.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.830
Narrator: But notoriously humid places,

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:47.100
such as Hong Kong and Louisiana,

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.090
have seen prominent transmissions.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:52.170
And experts warn that when large groups

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:53.660
begin to gather again,

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:56.320
we could actually see
another spike in cases,

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:57.810
which brings us back to the fact

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:01.260
that we may not see
seasonal patterns just yet.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:02.460
But this doesn't mean

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.443
that we won't see one in the future.

00:07:05.443 --> 00:07:07.650
If and when COVID-19 becomes

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:10.170
a more regular presence in populations,

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:14.120
there is a strong possibility
of it being seasonal.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.420
As immunity, vaccines, and treatments

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:18.320
become more readily available,

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.370
the susceptible pool will decrease,

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.030
and the spread will likely increase

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:24.300
only when the conditions
are more favorable

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.745
and therefore seasonal.

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:27.760
But, again, this is a new disease,

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:29.670
and these are only predictions.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.530
Current methods of stopping the outbreak

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.530
such as self-isolation
are far more efficient

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:37.488
than waiting for it to stop
naturally with the weather.

